{"It was unwise of Klein to play an old-fashioned type of opening, of which his great opponent is a past master." (AH Trott, BCM)} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Nc3 c6 8. e4 Qc7 9. Re1 Rd8 10. h3 a6 11. Qc2 e5 12. Be3 exd4 13. Nxd4 Nc5 14. Rad1 Bd7 15. g4 Be8 16. Bf4 Nfd7 17. Bg3 Ne5 18. Nce2 Qa5 19. f4 Ned7 20. Kh1 Nf8 21. Rf1 b5 22. a3 Qb6 23. cxb5 axb5 24. Bh4 Rdc8 25. Be7 Nb7 26. f5 c5 27. f6 cxd4 28. fxg7 Ne6 29. Qd2 Na5 30. Nxd4 $2 {"With an overwhelming game, [Euwe] was showing the onlookers the difference in style between a grand master and a master when ... he lost the exchange by an oversight." (Bruce Hayden, Sunday Times, 1 June 1952)} ({As Dr. Euwe afterwards pointed out} 30. Qh6 {wins, e.g.} Nc4 ({Klein, writing in BCM: "against 30 Qh6 I would have replied} 30... Qb7 {and obtained an overwhelming position within a few moves e.g.} 31. Nf4 {loses out of hand on account of} ({the only other moves,} 31. Bxd6 {or 31 Bf6 are met with} Bc6 {and ... White ... is compelled to defend the pawn with} 32. Ng3 {which in a few moves is doomed anyway. It is, nevertheless, a fact that in this position this move represented Dr. Euwe's best chance to save the game." (Klein)} ({Stockfish 17:} 32. Nf4 $5 Bxe4 33. Nxe6 Bxg2+ 34. Kh2 Bxf1 35. Rxf1 $11)) (31. Bf8 {(! - AHT)} Bc6 32. Ng3 Nc4 (32... Rxf8 33. gxf8=Q+ Rxf8 34. Rde1 Nc4 35. Nf5 $13) 33. Rf5 Rxf8 34. gxf8=Q+ Rxf8 35. Nh5 gxh5 36. Rg5+ Nxg5 37. Qxg5+ Kh8 38. Qf6+ Kg8 39. gxh5 $6) ({Stockfish 17:} 31. Bf6 Bc6 32. Kg1 Bxe4 33. Bxe4 Qxe4 34. Nxd4 $11) (31. Qh4 $5 Nc4 (31... Rc2 32. Nf4 Nxf4 33. Rxf4 Nc4 34. Bf6 g5 $1 35. Qxg5 Rxg2 36. Kxg2 Ne3+ 37. Kg1 Nxd1 38. Qh4 Bc6 39. g5 Bxe4 40. Rxe4 Nxb2 41. Re7 Qc6 42. g6 fxg6 43. Qg4 Qc4 44. Re2 $1 $18) 32. Nf4 Nxf4 33. Rxf4 Ne3 $13) 31... Nxf4 32. Rxf4 Qxe7 33. g5 Qe5 $19) (30... Nxg7 31. Bf6 Ne6 32. g5 Nb7 33. Rf4 {du Mont, The Field}) 31. Nf4 $1 Nxf4 32. Rxf4 {after which I can see nothing better for Black than} Ne3 33. g5 $1 {and if} Nxd1 34. Qxh7+ $1 Kxh7 35. Rh4+ Kxg7 36. Bf6+ Kf8 37. Rh8# {A very lucky escape for Klein - AHT}) ({"Even had Euwe not taken the pawn which lost him the exchange, playing, for instance,} 30. Bf6 $1 {, how is he to answer} Nc4 {... ?" (BH Wood, CHESS)} (30... Qb7 $2 31. Nxd4 Nc4 32. Qh6 Nxd4 33. Rxd4 $18) (30... Nb3 $5 31. Qh6 (31. Qb4 $5 Nbc5 32. Nxd4 Ra4 33. Qe1 Nxd4 (33... Nxg7 34. Qh4 Nd7 35. Bxg7 Kxg7 36. Ne6+ $1 fxe6 37. Qe7+ Kg8 38. Qxe6+ Kh8 39. Qe7 $18) 34. Bxd4 $16) 31... Qb7 32. Nf4 Bc6 33. Nd5 Bxd5 34. exd5 Rc2 35. Bf3 $16 {/+-}) {Stockfish 17:} 31. Qh6 Qc7 32. Nxd4 $18) 30... Qxd4 31. Qxd4 Nxd4 32. Rxd4 Nc6 33. Bf6 Nxd4 34. Bxd4 Ra4 35. Rd1 Rac4 36. Bc3 Rxc3 37. bxc3 Rxc3 38. e5 d5 ({"I missed a win, in extreme time pressure, with} 38... Kxg7 {" - Klein, CHESS, August 1952. Stockfish 17 continues:} 39. Rxd6 Re3 40. Kg1 Rxe5 41. Kf2 Rc5 {with a slight edge to Black.}) 39. Rxd5 Kxg7 40. Rd8 Bc6 41. Bxc6 Rxc6 42. Rd5 {Source: BCM, July 1952, p197 (annotations by AH Trott) & BCM, September 1952, p250 (Klein & Trott)} 1/2-1/2

