{"Klein's(?) Opening" (CHESS, August 1952)} 1. g3 d5 2. Bg2 Nf6 3. f4 (3. Nf3 {or 3 d4 are usual; as far as I know, 3 f4 is quite new but, if this game can be taken as any indication, a development along more traditional lines seem to be stronger - Barda}) 3... c5 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. c3 e6 6. d3 Qc7 7. O-O Be7 8. e4 ({This sacrifice looks interesting, but yields nothing in return. White's position would be stronger if the advance e4 could have been prepared by Qc2, Qe1 or Nbd2. But to} 8. Qc2 {Black answers} O-O {and after} 9. e4 c4 $1 {Barda}) (8. Qe1 {would be met by} b6 9. e4 dxe4 10. dxe4 Ba6) ({After} 8. Nbd2 Ng4 {would be uncomfortable for White.}) 8... dxe4 9. dxe4 Nxe4 {[d6 is a better square than f6 because the latter square is an ideal one for the black bishop; moreover White is now prevented from occupying c4 with the a1 knight (going via a3 or d2) - Barda]} 10. Ne5 Nd6 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Qa4 O-O ({Any effort by Black to retain his extra pawn by defending it with his bishop would get him into a cramped position - maybe dangerous too. At d7 or b7 the bishop would only block files along which the black rooks would operate. If White now takes back his sacrificed pawn, however, Black develops his pieces quickly and comfortably, for instance} 12... O-O 13. Qxc6 Qxc6 14. Bxc6 Rb8 15. Rf2 {(to free his bishop from the defence of the b2 pawn)} Bb7 16. Bxb7 Rxb7 {with pressure along the b-file and threats of ...Nf5, etc - Barda}) 13. Be3 Rb8 14. b3 ({Indirect defence of the c5 pawn. If} 14. Bxc5 {then} Rxb2 {and neither the a-pawn nor the c-pawn can be captured e.g.} 15. Qxc6 $2 (15. Bxa7 Bb7) (15. Bxc6 $2 Bd7 $1 16. Bxd6 Qxd6 17. Bxd7 Qd5 {etc}) 15... Rxg2+ $1) 14... Nb5 $1 15. Rc1 Rd8 16. Qe4 Rd5 17. Qc2 ({To} 17. c4 {the answer would be} Bf6 $1 {- BH Wood}) 17... Bb7 18. Qf2 ({Now, of course, if} 18. c4 $2 {there would have come} Nd4 {- BH Wood}) 18... Nd6 19. g4 ({White cannot take the rook or the pawn. If he plays} 19. Bxd5 {it is obvious that, after the reply} cxd5 {"something is going to happen" on the long white diagonal.}) ({After} 19. Bxc5 {either} Ne4 ({or} 19... Rxc5 20. Qxc5 Ne4 21. Qc4 {(what else?)} Qb6+) 20. Bxe4 Bxc5 {etc would win - Barda}) 19... f5 ({It may be that} 19... h5 {is stronger here. White cannot take the pawn and to play} 20. g5 {is to allow} Nf5) 20. c4 $1 Rd3 21. g5 Rd8 22. Nc3 Bc8 {[Black is preparing to play ...Ne4, force the exchange of this knight for White's strong king's bishop and open the c8-h3 diagonal. All this depends on the fact that White, to free his a1 rook, must move his c1 rook; and to free his c1 rook he must move his knight, thus leaving e4 only once guarded - Barda]} 23. Na4 Ne4 24. Bxe4 fxe4 {[It is rare to see such a strong position with five isolated pawns, four of them doubled! - Barda]} 25. Nb2 ({If} 25. Nc3 {then} e5 26. Nxe4 exf4 27. Bxf4 Qd7 {and Black has a fine game - Barda}) 25... R3d4 26. Re1 Rf8 27. Bxd4 Rxf4 28. Rxe4 ({Very interesting would have been} 28. Nd3 $1 {Black would then have two alternatives.} Rg4+ (28... Rxf2 29. Be5 $1 Bd6 30. Bxd6 Rf1+ 31. Kxf1 Qxd6 32. Nf2 Qxh2 33. Rxe4 e5 34. Rae1 Bh3+ 35. Nxh3 Qxh3+ {Black then wins the g5 pawn by another check and the united kingside pawns with the queen would be too much for White's rooks - Barda}) 29. Kh1 cxd4 30. Rf1 Bf6 31. gxf6 exd3 {(threatening c5 followed by Bb7+)} 32. Rg1 Rg6 {with a good game - Barda}) 28... Rxe4 ({If} 28... Rxf2 {then} 29. Bxf2 Bxg5 30. Rae1 Bf6 31. Nd3 {and White gets good play for his pieces - Barda}) 29. Rf1 Bd6 30. Be3 Bxh2+ 31. Kg2 ({After} 31. Qxh2 Qxh2+ 32. Kxh2 Rxe3 {with queens no longer on, correct play would win for Black - Barda}) 31... Bd6 32. Qf3 Rh4 33. Rh1 Rxh1 34. Kxh1 Qf7 35. Kg2 Bd7 36. Qd1 Qe7 37. Qf3 e5 38. Qe4 Qe6 {"The Radio Match arranged by the BBC between O Barda of Norway and E Klein, present British Champion, came to an abrupt end on May 30 when E Klein withdrew." Sources: CHESS, August 1952; BCM, July 1952, page 192.} (38... Qe6 {"At this point, Mr Klein found that he could not spare the time to conclude the game at the rate of a move per day but wanted to go on with it as a postal chess game, which could not be followed by the listeners. This suggestion was of course turned down by the Norwegian State Broadcasting System as well as by me, whereafter Mr Klein refused to play on. A most curious way of resigning! It is clear the game is lost for White. There might come} 39. Na4 {or 39 Nd3} Qh3+ 40. Kf2 Qh2+ 41. Qg2 (41. Ke1 Qxa2 {and wins}) 41... Qh4+ ({a long analysis shows a win after} 41... Qxg2+ {also - [Klein offered 50 pounds to play out the game after this move]}) 42. Qg3 {(White cannot allow ...e4)} Qe4 43. Bxc5 ({or} 43. Nxc5 Qc2+ 44. Kf1 Qd1+ 45. Qe1 Bh3+ 46. Kf2 Qg4 {and wins}) 43... Bxc5+ 44. Nxc5 Qd4+ 45. Qe3 Bf5 {with a won endgame - Barda, CHESS, August 1952, p222 [Klein offered 50 pounds to play out the game after this move]}) 0-1

